===== engine-readiness =====
measured-at: capture time, by this script, from the repository corpus
measurement-is-not-an-engine-query: the count and the key list below are
  what the engine is EXPECTED to load.  They are read from the tree, not
  from a running engine, and they are labelled declared-* for that reason.
measurement-command: find <repo> \( -type d \( -name target -o -name node_modules -o -name .git -o -name 'blitzy_adhoc_test_*' \) -prune \) -o \( -name '*.bpmn*' -type f -print \) | wc -l
measured-process-definitions: 36
second-measurement-command: find <repo> -name '*.bpmn*' -not -path '*/node_modules/*' | wc -l
measured-process-definitions-second-measurement: 36
expected-process-definitions: 36
measurement-basis: tracked paths enumerated by git ls-files, so no untracked tree beneath the repository root - a scratch checkout, an extracted archive, a dependency cache, a build copy, a deployment home - can move a corpus figure in either direction
two-measurements-because: a corpus size that matters is re-derived rather
  than copied, and two independent measurements that agree are worth more
  than one asserted number.  If they ever disagree, both are published with
  the instrument that produced each instead of one being chosen silently.
first-instrument: the tracked path set, base name matched against
  '*.bpmn*' by the shell -- the same matching find -name performs
second-instrument: git s own pathspec matching over the same tracked set,
  applied to the whole path with * crossing directory separators.  Two
  different matchers over one authoritative set: they can disagree about
  MATCHING and cannot disagree about MEMBERSHIP, which is the property that
  makes the agreement worth reporting.
instrument-that-was-withdrawn: a walk of the working tree.  It returned 794
  against a corpus of 36 in this environment, the extra 758 being build
  copies under target directories and a second checkout of the base commit.
  A measurement the environment can move that far corroborates nothing, so
  it is published in notes/corpus-figures.txt as a labelled diagnostic
  rather than used here as a second opinion.
declared-process-elements-total: 36
declared-process-definition-keys-distinct: 33
declared-process-definition-keys-declared-more-than-once: TaskBucketsSample TestActivitiSpringProcessUnitTest
why-total-and-distinct-differ: the list below has one entry per declaring
  file, so a key declared by more than one file appears more than once.  The
  repeats are named above rather than deduplicated away, because a key that
  two files declare is a property of the corpus a reader should see.
declared-definitions-declaring-a-script-task-or-script-format: 0
why-that-figure-matters: it is the first of the three findings that BOUND
  this flow risk without eliminating it.  With no script task and no script
  format anywhere in the corpus, the engine never asks the platform for a
  scripting engine, so the removal of the bundled one cannot reach it.  The
  other two findings are that the engine persistence layer reflects over the
  application OWN domain classes through ordinary unrestricted reflection,
  and that it reads no class bytes and touches no encapsulated platform
  package.  Bounded is not cleared.
